Output4Analyzing the Overlapping Subproblems in Egg Dropping PuzzleIn the recursive solution, many subproblems are computed more than once. Consider calculating the number of attempts for 2 eggs and 10 floors.When dropping an egg from a certain floor i, the problem divides into two subproblems: one for the floors below (eggDrop(1, i-1))one for the floors above (eggDrop(2, 10-i)).Different choices of i can lead to the same subproblem being solved multiple times (for example, eggDrop(2, 3) might be computed in several different branches).This repeated computation of the same subproblems demonstrates overlapping subproblems, which makes memoization a highly effective optimisation technique.[Better Approach] Using Memoization - O(n * k^2) Time and O(n * k) Space The above approach can be optimized using memoization, as we are computing the same sub-problem multiple times. The idea is to create a 2d array memo[][] of order n * k, to store the results of the sub-problem, where memo[i][j] stores the result of i eggs and j floors.  For each recursive call, check if the value is already computed, if so, return the stored value, else proceed similar to the above approach, and at last store the results in memo[][] and return the value.
